WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:09.490
Despite its recent resurgence

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:12.400
in the pop culture landscape, superheroes

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:14.120
and comic books have been with us

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:17.530
for over 80 years, and just like its host

00:00:17.530 --> 00:00:20.160
of larger-than-life
characters, the history

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:23.820
of comic books is
chock-full of wild stories.

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:25.630
They're sometimes more entertaining

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:27.650
than the heroes themselves.

00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:30.100
It also lets you see
what others don't, like

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:33.420
the irony of these two movie adaptations

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:35.980
opening only a month apart.

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:37.840
Though they might seem unrelated,

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:42.030
these two heroes share
a much longer history

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:45.220
and if it wasn't for a single lawsuit,

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:47.953
we might not have seen
either of them at all.

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:54.220
To tell this story, we need
to go back to where it began,

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:55.940
and it starts with one man

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:59.139
that you will recognize in an instant.

00:00:59.139 --> 00:01:00.642
- [Man] Up in the sky, look!

00:01:00.642 --> 00:01:01.602
- [Woman] It's a bird!

00:01:01.602 --> 00:01:02.557
- [Man] It's a plane!

00:01:02.557 --> 00:01:03.724
It's Superman!

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:07.230
- [Narrator] The arrival of
Superman on the comics scene

00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:09.160
was revolutionary.

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:13.560
His 1938 debut in "Action Comics
#1" essentially gave birth

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:16.210
to the superhero genre
and what is now referred

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:19.660
to as the Golden Age of Comics.

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:23.350
He was everywhere, in his
own national radio program,

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:25.950
newspaper comic strips, an animated short,

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:28.610
as well as a TV series.

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:33.100
It was shared in virtually
every possible format imaginable

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:34.900
except one:

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:36.010
movies.

00:01:36.010 --> 00:01:39.205
That's because someone
else took his place.

00:01:39.205 --> 00:01:41.617
- And now my son, repeat my name

00:01:41.617 --> 00:01:44.553
and return to the rescue of your friends.

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:47.373
- Shazam.

00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:52.933
- [Narrator] Captain
Marvel was another hero

00:01:52.933 --> 00:01:55.920
that made his debut in "Whiz Comics #2"

00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:00.350
in the late 1939 published
by Fawcett Comics.

00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:02.316
It featured a 12-year-old orphaned

00:02:02.316 --> 00:02:04.241
newsboy named Billy Batson

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:06.790
who can transform into an adult superhero

00:02:06.790 --> 00:02:09.370
by shouting one word.

00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:12.590
Captain Marvel became an instant success,

00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:14.960
launching his own new independent series

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:18.030
"Captain Marvel Adventures,"
which would later go on

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:20.563
to sell more copies than "Superman."

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:25.180
In fact, for a while Captain
Marvel was the most popular

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:28.520
superhero of the time, so popular that it

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:31.430
became the first superhero
ever to be adapted

00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:33.140
into a motion picture.

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:35.330
Now, there's more story behind this.

00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:37.890
The studio behind the
film, Republic Pictures,

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:40.440
was originally working with DC,

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:43.340
or National Comics
Publications at the time,

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:46.170
to make a "Superman"
picture, but they already

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:49.790
had an existing cartoon deal
with Paramount Pictures,

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:52.810
which prohibited them
from signing another.

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:57.350
So Republic Pictures chose
"Captain Marvel" instead.

00:02:57.350 --> 00:03:00.100
DC was obviously not pleased.

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:02.340
Fawcett had already
published "Master Man,"

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:05.420
an obvious copy of "Superman"
that they stopped publishing

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:07.903
after DC had threatened with lawsuit.

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:10.720
They believed "Captain Marvel" was

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:13.750
just another imitation as well.

00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:17.610
In June 1941, National
Comics finally sued Fawcett

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:19.710
for copyright infringement.

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:22.060
This began National Comics Publications

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:25.500
versus Fawcett Publications,
one of the longest-running

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:28.070
legal battles in the
history of comic books,

00:03:28.070 --> 00:03:32.270
lasting over 12 years.
Here's what happened.

00:03:32.270 --> 00:03:36.270
DC's argument was simple, that
Captain Marvel's main powers

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:39.470
and characteristics were
too similar to Superman

00:03:39.470 --> 00:03:42.023
and therefore infringed on its copyright.

00:03:43.130 --> 00:03:45.530
Fawcett argued that although
these two characters

00:03:45.530 --> 00:03:49.150
were similar, it wasn't to
the point of infringement

00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:52.010
and that similar feats
have already been performed

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:55.447
by other fictional characters
like Popeye or Tarzan.

00:03:56.587 --> 00:03:58.020
To prove their point, National Comics

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:02.370
prepared a binder over 150 pages in length

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:05.590
with panels from their comics
of "Superman" juxtaposed

00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:08.700
with similar panels of "Captain Marvel."

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:12.460
Everything was in close
scrutiny, from their costumes,

00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:16.280
boots, capes, the ability
to leap great distances,

00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:20.180
the ability to fly,
extraordinary strength and speed,

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:23.240
invincibility to bullets,
shells, explosives,

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:25.130
and their secret identity.

00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:26.960
They went as far as to include

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:30.290
that they both had clean-cut faces.

00:04:30.290 --> 00:04:32.780
The verdict was clear,
the judge eventually ruled

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:36.510
that "Captain Marvel" was
indeed a copy of "Superman,"

00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:38.893
but it was Fawcett who won the trial.

00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:43.370
It was all because of one tiny mistake,

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:46.440
a mistake that looked like this.

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:50.020
This was the copyright symbol
used back in the 1950s.

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:52.960
The lawyers from Fawcett
discovered that McClure Syndicate,

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:56.140
the newspaper company that
published the "Superman" comics,

00:04:56.140 --> 00:04:57.970
had forgotten to place these symbols

00:04:57.970 --> 00:05:00.030
on several of their strips

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:04.100
and argued that DC had no
copyright to "Superman,"

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:06.010
and the court agreed.

00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:08.320
This was devastating news for DC.

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:10.250
It meant that it did not own Superman

00:05:10.250 --> 00:05:13.010
and that anyone could
publish Superman's stories

00:05:13.010 --> 00:05:15.030
without legal repercussions.

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:17.590
This lasted over two years.

00:05:17.590 --> 00:05:20.340
Out of 160 strips of "Superman," published

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:23.590
from January 1939 to April 1950,

00:05:23.590 --> 00:05:27.040
less than half of them
were from Detective Comics.

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:30.270
DC immediately appealed,
and despite the damage,

00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:32.620
the decision was reversed.

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:34.295
The judge of the case, Learned Hand,

00:05:34.295 --> 00:05:35.870
declared "Captain Marvel"

00:05:35.870 --> 00:05:39.390
a deliberate and unabashed
duplicate of "Superman"

00:05:39.390 --> 00:05:42.030
and told Fawcett to cease
all of its publications

00:05:42.030 --> 00:05:44.790
and pay DC for the damage it owes.

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:46.750
Fawcett settled.

00:05:46.750 --> 00:05:51.090
By 1950, the Golden Age of
Comic Books had come to an end.

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:53.290
Sales were slumping to all-time lows,

00:05:53.290 --> 00:05:57.760
and Fawcett simply decided
that it wasn't worth fighting.

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:02.110
They paid $400,000 in
damages, almost $4 million

00:06:02.110 --> 00:06:05.530
by today's standards,
and with its last issue,

00:06:05.530 --> 00:06:08.623
it seemed like the end
for "Captain Marvel."

00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:11.730
As you well know, it was not.

00:06:11.730 --> 00:06:14.360
A brand-new publication
that no one knew at the time

00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:17.800
trademarked the name after
it was abandoned by Fawcett.

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:20.693
That publication was called Marvel.

00:06:20.693 --> 00:06:23.190
It took repeating slumps in sales

00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:26.820
and six different iterations
until Carol Danvers,

00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:29.250
whom most of us are now familiar with,

00:06:29.250 --> 00:06:32.873
took on the mantle of
Captain Marvel in 1977.

00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:36.730
Meanwhile, DC Comics,
looking for a new hero

00:06:36.730 --> 00:06:39.030
to add to their lineup, bought the rights

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:42.270
to all of Fawcett's superheroes in 1972,

00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:45.310
including the original Captain Marvel.

00:06:45.310 --> 00:06:48.570
The irony was that DC couldn't
call him Captain Marvel

00:06:48.570 --> 00:06:52.150
because Marvel had the name
and decided to name him

00:06:52.150 --> 00:06:54.140
after the magic catchphrase

00:06:54.140 --> 00:06:56.463
that turns him into a superhero.

00:06:57.300 --> 00:07:00.630
And in 2019, these two
movies are being released

00:07:00.630 --> 00:07:03.770
just a month apart from one another.

00:07:03.770 --> 00:07:05.670
The National Comics Publications

00:07:05.670 --> 00:07:09.540
versus Fawcett Publications
is a prime example

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:13.520
of the thin line between
plagiarism and inspiration,

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:16.040
and the tricky balance
that all artists had

00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:20.130
to find in the early years,
a growing pain of sorts.

00:07:20.130 --> 00:07:22.680
If it hadn't been for
Superman, a revolution

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:26.490
in pop culture history, we
wouldn't have had Shazam,

00:07:26.490 --> 00:07:28.750
and if it hadn't been
for Shazam, we would not

00:07:28.750 --> 00:07:30.920
have had Captain Marvel.

00:07:30.920 --> 00:07:33.030
Ironically enough, Superman, as we know,

00:07:33.030 --> 00:07:35.810
might not have existed
without Shazam as well.

00:07:35.810 --> 00:07:37.200
During the first few years,

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:39.630
"Superman" also drew lots of inspiration

00:07:39.630 --> 00:07:41.910
from the original "Captain Marvel,"

00:07:41.910 --> 00:07:46.380
like how his bald, mad scientist
archnemesis Doctor Sivana

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:49.240
led to Lex Luthor, and as for Superman

00:07:49.240 --> 00:07:51.060
having the gift of flight,

00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:55.020
it was Captain Marvel who
performed that feat first.

00:07:55.020 --> 00:07:58.470
A Superman who can't fly,
can you imagine that?

00:07:58.470 --> 00:08:01.150
Neither can I, and that alone might

00:08:01.150 --> 00:08:03.813
be a good enough reason
why we need Shazam.

